Section 111.4: Virtual functions
Virtual Methods are methods in Java that are non-static and without the keyword Final in front. All methods by
default are virtual in Java. Virtual Methods play important roles in Polymorphism because children classes in Java
can override their parent classes' methods if the function being overridden is non-static and has the same method
signature.
There are, however, some methods that are not virtual. For example, if the method is declared private or with the
keyword ﬁnal, then the method is not Virtual.
Consider the following modiﬁed example of inheritance with Virtual Methods from this StackOverﬂow post How do
virtual functions work in C# and Java? :
public class A{
    public void hello(){
        System.out.println("Hello");
    }

    public void boo(){
        System.out.println("Say boo");
    }
}

